# UI styling (Bootstrap skins)

We build white-label products that are often skinned to customer visuals: logos, fonts, colors, rounding, borders, etc. Skins are Bootstrap-based CSS files fetched from CDN; skin choice is configurable in many apps.

Bootstrap is our shared vocabulary — known by designers, usable in Vue, React, and plain HTML.

## Guidelines

- **Use Bootstrap styles** unless otherwise instructed.
  - Not shadcn, not Tailwind, and avoid custom CSS when a utility class exists.
- **Model UI from Bootstrap components first** (card, list-group, alert, btn, etc.).
- **Add a root `q-` class** on components that skins may override, e.g. `q-plugin-tool-group` for `PluginToolGroup`.
- **Custom styling that skins should override:**
  - Put it in a single shared CSS file (e.g. `ui-plugin.css`) imported in Storybook preview and app bootstrap — **not** in production library bundles.
  - These rules are ported to the skins project at release.
  - When colors are needed in custom CSS, use Bootstrap CSS variables (e.g. `var(--bs-primary)`, `var(--bs-body-color)`) — not hardcoded hex/rgb values.
- **Non-overridable layout** (position, display, link underline removal): prefer Bootstrap utility classes (`d-flex`, `text-decoration-none`, …).
- **Never use Vue `<style scoped>`** in component libraries.
- **Default skin:** `https://cdn.quartal.com/skins/default.css` — load via `loadDefaultSkin()` or equivalent in Storybook and dev apps.
